r by ten hundred thousand so it's  like moving the decimal place you know  one place each time you move to the  right or the left and we'll take a look  at that in some of the examples and then  some other simple ones are like one mile  is 5,280 feet and one pound is 16 ounces  and so on and so forth so some of these  common ones you're going to want to  memorize let's get right into the unit  versions now so how do you convert from  12 feet per second two miles per hour  it's a good question right so let's see  so first thing we want to do is want to  get from feet to miles okay so let's see  so basically what we have here is we  have 5280 feet for every one mile now  the question is is where do we put the  feet and where do we put the miles what  you want to do is you want to make sure  that the feet which are the units that  you don't want are going to cancel  numerator and denominator so you don't  want the feet both in the numerator you  want one of the numerator one of the  dynamic so they're going to cancel each  other out and that's equal to one mile  ok now we want to get rid of the seconds  we want to convert from seconds to hours  now you might want to convert from  seconds to minutes first and then  minutes to hours so let's do it like  that so what we have is there's 60  seconds for every one minute and the  reason I set it up this way and not the  reciprocal way is because i want the  seconds to cancel with a second  numerator and denominator then for every  minute i'm sorry for every 60 minutes  there's one hour so you can see again  numerator and denominator canceling now  you know your you've got it right  because at the very end you can see that  we have miles of the numerator see miles  hours in the denominator and that's  miles per hour miles / hours so all it's  left to do now is multiply all the  numerators together all the denominators  together and then divide and you've got  the number of miles per hour so let's go  to the next example the n